exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′}+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-3(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+(2-3)\\beta {e}^{2x} & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x}-\\beta {e}^{2x} & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-1,\\qquad -\\beta =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-1,\\qquad \\beta =-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0-3 & =-1 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531842 1080 "2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}" 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531842 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531842 1083 #uuid 