\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{6x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{6x}) & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(6+5)\\beta {e}^{6x} & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+11\\beta {e}^{6x} & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12,\\qquad 11\\beta =-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12,\\qquad \\beta =-\\frac{6}{11}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0-\\frac{6}{11} & =8 \\\\ A & =\\frac{94}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{94}{11}{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$" 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531846 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531844 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531844 1077 "field-1" 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531844 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531846 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531844 1079 #uuid "2bf99e47-670f-558a-9e23-5e7fa9452cc5" 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531845 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531851 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531845 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531845 1077 "field-1" 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531845 1078 #edb/ref 17592186531851 13194139535838 17592186045443 true] [17592186531845 1079 #uuid "2e84de7c-53fc-5f01-bfdc